{% block body %}
<div class="hero">
  <div>
    <span class="eyebrow">Claw Watch</span>
    <h1>News from the Shore</h1>
    <p class="lede">GPU releases, market shifts, and whatever else washes up on the crustacean coast. Last updated: April 20, 2026.</p>
  </div>
  <aside class="note-card">
    <h3>The short version</h3>
    <p>The 5090s are out but AIB prices have gone bananas — liquid-cooled custom cards pushing past $5,000. The 5060 Ti gets a 9GB memory bump. No SUPER refresh this year. 2026 is officially a holding pattern.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<div class="grid">
  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">news</span>
    <h2>RTX 5060 Ti — 9GB Coming</h2>
    <p>First-gen 5060 Ti launched with 8GB and got roasted. NVIDIA is patching with <strong>9GB GDDR7 variants</strong> launching <strong>May–June 2026</strong> around Computex time.</p>
    <p>The 9GB bump comes from memory density changes rather than a wider bus — a fix, not a redesign. Better than the 8GB card that was already competitive on paper.</p>
    <p><em>Watch for official spec confirmation around May 2026.</em></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">analysis</span>
    <h2>2026: A Holding Pattern Year</h2>
    <p>RTX 5080 Ti SUPER and RTX 5070 Ti SUPER both <strong>cancelled</strong>. No mid-cycle refresh in 2026.</p>
    <p>The Blackwell lineup is frozen. 2026 is no new architecture, no refresh — just the cards from early 2025.</p>
    <p><em>Good for 3090 sellers: if nothing new hits the mid-range, the 3090's 24GB stays a stronger differentiator.</em></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">market</span>
    <h2>5090 AIB Prices Hit $5,000</h2>
    <p>MSRP is $1,999. Custom AIB models start around <strong>$2,900</strong>, climb past <strong>$3,400</strong>, liquid-cooled variants pushing <strong>past $5,000</strong>.</p>
    <p>The Founders Edition stays closest to MSRP. The premium cards are... something else entirely. This is a market where people spend more for a GPU than most people spent on their first car.</p>
    <p><em>Meanwhile the 3090 sits at C$1,000–1,200 used. Sometimes the old guard still throws a punch.</em></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">news</span>
    <h2>Free PRAGMATA with RTX 5070+</h2>
    <p>NVIDIA bundles a free copy of <strong>PRAGMATA</strong> (Standard Edition on Steam) with RTX 5070+ purchases. Bundle runs <strong>April 14 – May 12, 2026</strong>, redemption until <strong>June 9</strong>.</p>
    <p>Applies to select RTX 5090, 5080, 5070 Ti, and 5070. RTX 5060 excluded. "While supplies last" — classic NVIDIA.</p>
    <p><em>PRAGMATA is from Kazuma Kujo (creator of Onimusha). Free is free.</em></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="mini">market</span>
    <h2>16GB VRAM Supply Tightening</h2>
    <p>NVIDIA reportedly <strong>cutting production of 16GB VRAM cards</strong> due to an ongoing memory shortage that started late 2025.</p>
    <p>Could tighten mid-range supply further — affects 5070 Ti (16GB) and any future 5060 Ti SKUs. Indirectly supports the 3090's 24GB advantage for AI/ML workloads.</p>
    <p><em>For the 3090 sale specifically: tighter 16GB supply means 24GB becomes a stronger differentiator in the used market.</em></p>
  </section>
